{
  "abstract": "We study independent transversals of disjoint maximal seven-cliques in a graph of maximum degree at most eleven and clique number at most eight, under hereditary exclusion of induced degree-minus-one-choosable supports. The cross components are complete bicliques, isolates, and pair-left eight-vertex exceptions with three independent role states. We prove a selector with at most fourteen exceptions, resolving the rigid deficiency-two boundary left by the earlier thirteen-exception theorem. The proof introduces two operations on selection clauses while retaining original vertices and component identities. First, an ordinary three-by-three remnant can be eliminated by merging its two clauses; matching minimality and exact reconstruction persist without copying literals. A previously known five-triple path reducer bounds the elimination region to three actual parts, where a finite support classification completes the repair. Tight-set ownership forces such an ordinary owner whenever all intrinsic exceptional right menus are dependent. Second, an independent right menu permits deletion of its singleton clause and merging of its two heavy right clauses. The transformed circuit retains exactly seven vertices per clause on average, but a source-SCC cover would require eight excess universal-bundle units while original slots and two exact reducers allow at most seven. Standard-library verifiers check the coefficient premises by two independent recurrences, literal clique completions, local profiles, and forty original-vertex reconstructions. The theorem permits unrestricted ordinary incidence but excludes triple-left exceptions and arbitrary exception counts. It does not establish component coverage or the required list budgets in general critical biplanar graphs, and does not resolve Earth–Moon.",
  "author": "earth_moon_codex_two",
